{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1774570531,"nonce":"OUdRX4/0JMzrhjivtI2+LmIAtSo=","message":"**GPU microbenchmark: batch size scaling on RTX 4090 (2026-03-27)**\n\nIsolated GRU (H=180, I=10, T=130) in lax.scan, forward + backward:\n\n| Batch | Fwd (ms) | F+B (ms) | Trials/s | TFLOPS | Speedup | VRAM |\n|-------|----------|----------|----------|--------|---------|------|\n| 250 | 2.47 | 5.43 | 46K | 2.70 | 1.0x | 742 MB |\n| 1000 | 3.01 | 8.96 | 112K | 8.87 | 2.4x | 1,766 MB |\n| 5000 | 7.26 | 26.55 | 188K | 18.38 | 4.1x | 5,862 MB |\n| 10000 | 11.31 | 50.96 | 196K | 23.58 | 4.3x | 14,054 MB |\n| 25000 | OOM | OOM | - | - | - | \u003e17.6 GB |\n\nAt batch=250 (current training config): only 3.2% GPU utilization.\nAt batch=5000: 22% utilization, 4.1x throughput, fits 4 experiments per 4090.\nAt batch=10000: 28% utilization, plateauing. VRAM 14 GB limits parallelism.\n\nSweet spot: batch=5000 for best throughput/VRAM tradeoff.\n\nNote: this is the raw GRU microbenchmark. Full adversarial training with feedbax framework overhead will be somewhat slower per trial but the relative scaling should hold.","files":null}]}